Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="war-medicine"),{"src": "war-medicine.m4a", "embedUrl": "", "shareUrl": "", "approved": true, "creator": "Nizar Jaber", "duration": "35:27", "durationSeconds": 2126.6, "language": "English and Arabic", "poster": "nizar-cover.png", "posterAlt": "A crowd and an ambulance outside the American University of Beirut Medical Center at night.", "coverCaption": "People gathered outside the American University of Beirut Medical Center during the September 2024 pager explosions.", "coverCredit": "Reuters", "synopsis": "War Medicine in Lebanon: Ethics, Practice, and the Limits of Care in Times of Crisis is an oral-history podcast examining what happens to medicine when the systems that normally support care are disrupted by war. Drawing on interviews with surgeons, a medical student, and a civilian affected by the September 2024 pager explosions, the podcast explores how scarcity, mass casualties, danger, and damaged infrastructure reshape clinical practice. It considers the ethical challenges of triage and resource allocation, the moral burden placed on healthcare workers, the disruption of medical education, and the difficulty of preserving patients’ narratives during emergencies.", "statement": "This podcast began with a central question: What happens to medicine when the system it depends on begins to collapse? Rather than treating war medicine as a separate medical specialty, I wanted to explore how ordinary medicine itself changes when its usual conditions—time, resources, safety, infrastructure, and continuity—can no longer be assumed.\nI chose an oral-history podcast because personal testimony allows these changes to be examined through the experiences of people occupying different positions within the same crisis. Interviews with physicians explore triage, scarcity, moral responsibility, and the limits of care; a medical student's perspective examines how war disrupts education and shapes professional identity; and a civilian perspective asks whether the patient's story can still be heard when hospitals are overwhelmed by urgent injury.\nUltimately, the project argues that war medicine represents a structural transformation of medical practice. At the same time, examining how medicine adapts to war should not normalize the conditions that make such adaptation necessary. The medical humanities provide a way to understand these experiences not only through clinical outcomes, but through ethics, narrative, education, and human experience.", "credits": "Nizar Jaber — Creator, interviewer, narrator/producer.\nDr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah — Interviewee; plastic and reconstructive surgeon discussing war medicine and conflict-zone practice.\nDr. Alaa Haydar — Interviewee; general surgeon discussing medical practice during the 2024 war in South Lebanon.\nMohamad Jaber — Interviewee; AUB medical student discussing medical education during war.\nAnonymous civilian affected by the September 2024 pager explosions — Interviewee providing the patient/civilian perspective.\n\nInterview credits\nInterview with Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah, conducted by Nizar Jaber, May 12, 2026.\nInterview with Dr. Alaa Haydar, conducted by Nizar Jaber, May 6, 2026.\nInterview with Mohamad Jaber, conducted by Nizar Jaber, May 4, 2026.\nAnonymous civilian interview, conducted by Nizar Jaber, May 5, 2026.\n\nCover photograph: Reuters.", "transcript": "War Medicine in Lebanon: Ethics, Practice, and the Limits of Care in Times of Crisis\nPodcast Transcript\nCreator and Host: Nizar Jaber\n[0:00] Nizar Jaber: Medicine is often imagined as a structured and controlled practice. We imagine hospitals, protocols, supplies, consultations, operating rooms, and ethical principles that guide the doctor-patient relationship. But war disrupts the conditions that make this kind of medicine possible. In Lebanon, this is not an abstract question. The healthcare system has repeatedly been tested by crisis. The 2006 July war, the Beirut port explosion in 2020, the 2024 escalation in south of Lebanon, the major explosions, and the ongoing pressure of the 2026 war. Each of these events placed hospitals, physicians, nurses, students, and patients in situations where ordinary medical routines were interrupted by mass casualties, damaged infrastructure, displacements, and shortages. This podcast asks a simple but difficult question. What happens to medicine when the system it depends on begins to collapse? I argue that under conditions of war and crisis, medicine does not simply adapt. It undergoes a structural transformation, ethical priorities shift, clinical decision-making becomes more collective, medical education is disrupted and reorganized, and patient narratives are often compressed by urgency. Reports from the World Health Organization and Reuters show how Lebanon's health system has faced repeated pressure from mass injury, hospital disruption, attacks on emergency responders, and shortages of medical supplies. To understand war medicine, we first need to move beyond the idea that it's simply emergency medicine performed faster. War medicine is a form of care shaped by scarcity, danger, disrupted infrastructure. Médecins Sans Frontières describes war surgery as a medicine practiced with limited resources in conflict settings. This means that clinical decisions are not shaped only by diagnosis, but also by what is available. Who can be treated, who can be transferred, and how many patients arriving at once. Mark Harrison's history of wartime medicine also shows that this is not new. Across different wars, medicine has repeatedly been reorganized around the needs of collective survival. War pushes medicine away from the ideal of unlimited individualized care towards the difficult question of how to use the limited sources for the greatest possible benefit. This historical dimension also matters because war medicine has never been only about survival. The facial prosthetic masks used after World War I remind us that war medicine also deals with the body after survival. Visible injury, identity, reconstruction, and social return. To explore this, I spoke with four people. A civilian injured during the crisis and the major explosions, Dr. Ghassan Abu Sitta, Dr. Alaa Haydar, and a medical student at AUB. Together, their testimonies move from the patient's experience to the surgeon's ethical burden, to the local practice of medicine under war, and finally to what medical education should learn from this experience. I begin with the civilian perspective because war medicine is not only about physicians and hospitals, it is also about the person who suddenly becomes a patient inside the system under pressure.\n[3:23] Nizar Jaber: So, for our first question, it would be, could you briefly please introduce yourself and describe only as much as you feel comfortable what happened to you in the September 2024 major explosions that happened in Lebanon? What do you remember most about seeking or receiving care? After the event and staying at the hospital when you were injured?\n[4:34] Anonymous Civilian Interviewee: After the injury, I can't remember specifically, I remember it was important that the injury happened at about 4 p.m. At that time, the major bomb came from my relatives, but it was crowded in all hospitals for blood donations, taking care of the patients, and the severity of the injuries of different types.\n[5:20] Nizar Jaber: So, what was the hardest part of that experience for you? Was it the fear, the waiting, the confusion, or the hospital environment or something else?\n[5:25] Anonymous Civilian Interviewee: First of all, it wasn't about you specifically, it was about what happened. We couldn't understand what happened at first because it was first of a kind, such huge injuries happen for many people, but then after understanding the situation a bit, we could start thinking about how should we deal with the injury, how we will be viewed by other people in the community regarding our injuries, how could we adapt to them in our daily lives.\n[6:02] Nizar Jaber: Did you feel that things were explained to you clearly and what was happening to you at the hospital, that you were treated as a person or just as an injury and the case that has to proceed?\n[6:13] Anonymous Civilian Interviewee: Well, the healthcare system, the nurses in the hospitals, doctors, everyone did an amazing job because such a crisis is taking place with so this amount of injuries, this amount of different injuries in the eyes, hands, and different body organs, doing their best to help keep alive this large number of people is a huge achievement for the medical care system in Lebanon.\n[6:43] Nizar Jaber: So, looking back to that event, what do you wish healthcare workers understood more about the patient experience during a crisis like this?\n[6:48] Anonymous Civilian Interviewee: First of all, we can't really name healthcare workers for anything in this domain because it's first time in history, or in modern history, at least such a huge event happens, but for next time as an advice for any upcoming crisis, hope none of this happens again, that the healthcare workers should listen more to patients' needs, what do they need, maybe this will help them in order to treat their injuries in a better way.\n[7:24] Nizar Jaber: This testimony reminds us that injury is not a biological event. In class, we discussed the distinction between disease and illness, where disease refers to the bodily dysfunction, while illness refers to the lived experience of suffering. In this case, the medical system may see wounds, urgency, and treatment priorities, but the patient remembers fear, confusion, waiting, and the need to feel recognized. This is where narrative medicine becomes important. Rita Charon argues that medicine requires the ability to listen and interpret stories of illness. In war, this becomes harder because time is limited and hospitals are overwhelmed, but it also becomes more necessary because patients risk becoming anonymous cases in a mass casualty setting. The patient's story shows what war feels like from the receiving end of care. Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah's account then shows what war demands from the physician. Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah is a surgeon whose work in conflict settings provide a broader view of war medicine. I asked him how medicine changes when it enters wartime conditions and how scarcity affects surgical decision making. My question would be if you can briefly begin by introducing your specialty and the main conflict and war settings that you've been involved in or worked in.\n[8:42] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: My name is Ghassan Abu-Sittah. I'm a plastic and reconstructive surgeon and the professor of conflict medicine at the American University of Beirut. I have 38-ish. Since 89 to today, I have been involved during different parts of my career in conflict medicine from the Palestinian First Intifada in 89 and then the first American war on Iraq, then Grapes of Wrath in Lebanon in 96, then the Second Intifada, then the 2009 war on Gaza, then the 2012 war on Gaza, and then the Syrian Civil War, and then the war in Mosul, and the 2014 war in Gaza, then the Marches of Return in Gaza, then the war in Yemen, and then the war in 2021 in Gaza, and then the 2023 war, and then the 24 war in Lebanon and this war.\n[10:05] Nizar Jaber: I ask you what changes first when medicine enters wartime conditions, the difference between the classical, theoretical, ideal practice medicine versus when we have war conditions.\n[10:19] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: So first, resources invariably are less than need, and so you need to triage not just the patients, but also you need to triage the resources. Now that differs from different wars, and so some wars, like the genocide in Gaza, were under very austere conditions. Other wars, less austere conditions in terms of supplies. And so what is critical in managing wars is to assess your capabilities and the demand of the war to be able to best manage your health system during the war.\n[11:09] Nizar Jaber: How does war change the surgical decision making, especially knowing you're a surgeon? So when it comes to surgery, how does this shift happen?\n[11:19] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: So first of all, we need to understand that war injuries are a very different form of trauma than civilian injuries. The amount of energy into the tissues, the amount of tissue devitalization, the amount of polytrauma, it's a different injury, and that's one of the biggest problems that happens in countries that don't have experience with war, that surgeons start treating these injuries as they would any other traumatic injury. So the decision making is related to the nature of the injuries and to the triage system. Who are you going to prioritize? Who's going to go into the operating room? And how often you need to take people. The difference between a traumatic injury that's a civilian injury, like a road traffic accident, and a blast injury is that you need to take the blast injury back to the OR two or three times before you can even consider reconstruction. And so it said the management of the patient is different and the management of your resources are different.\n[12:32] Nizar Jaber: So how does this affect the relationship between the patient and the doctor?\n[12:37] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: The patient and the doctor. Triage is about managing your resources. And so on Wednesday, when Israel bombed over 100 sites in Lebanon, we received hundreds of patients. And therefore, it was critical for us to decide who needs to go to the OR now, who can be stabilized and sent to the wards, and who needs to go back in the days after. And therefore, the relationship is with the incident, not with the individual patient. You manage the incident as a surgeon. You don't manage the patient. And that's how you provide the best possible treatment. You can apply the three principles, you know, first life-saving, then limb-saving, and then you treat the others.\n[13:37] Nizar Jaber: On a personal level, how do you process witnessing such large number of injured civilians, especially when you work with children? So as we know, always doctors are on the edge of having burnout and moral injury. So these events, do they make you stop or make you want to pursue medicine more?\n[13:55] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: I think it's critical. And I think newer generations of health professionals are understanding the need to look after themselves in terms of providing mental health support. So currently, our colleagues in the Pediatric Intensive Care Unit, who deal with our children, the most critically ill children, now have mental health support. All of the members of our team have twice-weekly mental health sessions, just to kind of prevent that kind of burnout. And I think that's critical, to be able to stand back and understand that even as the health provider, as the doctor, you are being injured through the process of treatment.\n[14:48] Nizar Jaber: The patterns that you have seen, were they repeated across the different conflict zones or somewhere more severe and more dangerous?\n[15:01] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: Each war has a signature injury pattern that helps you understand the aims of the war. After the ceasefire in Lebanon, we started seeing a different pattern of injury, where the injuries were more frequent and they're more severe, because Israel decided to ethnically cleanse the South, and therefore started systematically destroying people's homes. And so the wounded were coming in from underneath the rubble of their homes in the South. And the majority of the kids had lost parents and siblings. Before the war, there were more a mixture of shrapnel and blast and crush injuries under the rubble. Gaza was different, closer to what we're witnessing now, but on a larger scale. Mosul was much more gunshot wounds and IEDs. Syria was much more gunshot wounds. And every war, you kind of get to figure out what the main pattern of injuries are.\n[16:16] Nizar Jaber: Besides the clinician role, during war times, does the physician become only a clinician or also he's a witness of what is happening?\n[16:23] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: No, I think you're morally bound to bear witness and speak out on behalf of the victims, your patients, who are unable to speak out.\n[16:36] Nizar Jaber: Yeah, because I wanted to tell you that during, as you said, in the war, the patient can't speak out. And as you know, the patient narrative is really important. So do you feel like the physician after talking, he reinforces the patient's narrative? And is this part of the physician's duty? Or do you feel like we have other sides that should contribute and help?\n[16:57] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: No, I think as a physician, you should be the patient advocate. And you need to advocate for your patient in speaking up against what are the crimes of assault against them.\n[17:14] Nizar Jaber: Okay, for our last question is just to tackle the medical education aspect. So do you feel like new medical students should understand something that they don't learn about war medicine? And how do you feel like they can be prepared to what is happening in our country?\n[17:30] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: There is no doubt that one of the biggest failures of medical education in the Arab world is the failure to integrate what is part of the surgical load that every doctor in the Arab world will deal with at some stage in their career, whether they're in Iraq or Yemen or Lebanon or Libya or Sudan, or now even in the Gulf. Because war injuries are almost an endemic disease in the region. Yet, because our curricula are copied verbatim from the Global North teaching institutions, we don't prepare our doctors for this surgical disease.\n[18:21] Nizar Jaber: So do you feel like they're not equipped enough? If, for example, crisis conditions happen, they can't respond?\n[18:28] Dr. Ghassan Abu-Sittah: I can tell you even surgical residents are not. A lot of countries, not even the surgical residents are taught conflict or war surgery.\n[18:41] Nizar Jaber: What is important in Dr. Abu-Sittah's account is that he does not describe war medicine as something limited to one country or one crisis. By referring to different conflict zones, he shows that certain patterns repeat. Hospitals become overwhelmed, surgical decisions are shaped by scarcity, and physicians are forced to make decisions that ordinary medical training does not fully prepare them for. His point about medical students and surgical residents is especially important. Medical education often prepares students for medicine in relatively stable systems. Hospitals with staff, supplies, protocols, imaging, operating rooms, and referral pathways. War exposes the limits of that assumption. It asks physicians and trainees to think not only clinically, but also ethically, logistically, and politically. This does not mean that medical students should be expected to function as war doctors. Rather, it means that they should be taught how medicine changes under crisis, how triage works, how scarcity shapes care, how moral injury develops, and how physicians can remain humane when the system around them is failing. But to understand how these pressures appear in Lebanon itself, I now turn to Dr. Alaa Haydar, whose experience brings the discussion from the broader structure of conflict medicine to the daily reality of surgical practice in South Lebanon during the war.\n[20:12] Nizar Jaber: In short, about your specialization, where were you working during the war in South Lebanon?\n[20:19] Dr. Alaa Haydar: I am a general surgeon, with a focus on obesity surgery. I completed my training around three years ago, and I have been working at Sheikh Ragheb Hospital during the war.\n[20:38] Nizar Jaber: How did your medical practice change between wartime conditions and ordinary days?\n[20:43] Dr. Alaa Haydar: In ordinary days, most of the operations we do are elective, or what we call “cold surgeries.” These include procedures like gallbladder surgery, sleeve gastrectomy, and gastric bypass. The patient is usually stable. They do not come in with major trauma, shock, or severe bleeding.During the war, it was completely different. We started seeing cases that we do not usually encounter in daily practice. The protocols changed toward damage-control surgery. For example, depending on the injury, we might be dealing with splenic or liver trauma, blunt abdominal trauma, or severe lower-limb injuries. In some cases, the patient might require amputation, vascular surgery, grafting, or bypass. Many of these patients had lost a large amount of fluids or blood. So, patients during war are very different from the patients we usually see in ordinary days.\n[22:00] Nizar Jaber: What were the main constraints you faced: supplies, staff, or safety?\n[22:05] Dr. Alaa Haydar: During war, because of the large number of patients, especially in a hospital in South Lebanon that is very close to the airstrikes, you can face many difficulties. One major difficulty can be blood transfusion. Sometimes you may not be able to provide the exact blood type the patient needs, and even O-negative blood may not always be available. So the blood bank becomes a very important limitation.The same applies to medical supplies. It depends on what the hospital has stored and what its capacity is. We had calculated what we might need: nylon sutures, Vicryl sutures, and emergency materials. Another issue is fatigue and exhaustion, depending on how many days the war continues. But in terms of supplies, in our hospital, we did not really have a shortage since we were prepared if the war takes time.\n[23:15] Nizar Jaber: Were there specific moments when you had to modify your standards of care?\n[23:20] Dr. Alaa Haydar: Of course. When the patient arrives at the emergency department, they may be in a state of shock. At that moment, we may not yet know whether there is an abdominal injury that requires laparotomy, or whether there is a severe colonic injury.In these situations, we may perform something like a Hartmann’s procedure: we remove the injured segment of colon and create a colostomy instead of reconnecting the bowel immediately. Later, once the patient is stabilized and has recovered from the resuscitation phase, we can consider restoring bowel continuity with an anastomosis.The same applies for pancreatic cases, where the focus becomes concentrated on damage-control surgeries. The goal of damage-control surgery is to avoid worsening the patient’s condition. We try to control bleeding and contamination quickly, instead of doing a long definitive operation while the patient is unstable. At the same time, we continue resuscitation: giving fluids, blood products when available, correcting acidosis, and trying to prevent the patient from entering the cycle of acidosis, hypothermia, and coagulopathy. Then we perform the surgery.\n[25:30] Nizar Jaber: Did working under these conditions lead to burnout or moral distress among physicians and healthcare workers?\n[25:36] Dr. Alaa Haydar: Yes, exactly. During war, especially at night, patients may arrive at the same time. You could be in the operating room performing a laparotomy, and then another patient that also needs a laparotomy arrives at the emergency department at the same moment. The amount of patients creates exhaustion and pressure on the medical team. But when you see these kinds of cases, the medical mission becomes very clear: you do as much as you can to help, and you try to save the patient as quickly as possible.\n[26:17] Nizar Jaber: What did this experience teach you about the limits of medicine, and what should future doctors know to prepare for war medicine?\n[26:28] Dr. Alaa Haydar: The first thing I want to say is that, in medicine, we must always remember that medicine is a human and ethical mission. This is something that should remain at the core of medical practice. These are the basic values that we learn during our medical training, and future doctors need to understand that they carry a responsibility. They have a duty to provide care, especially in situations like wars, disasters, and crises, where doctors are on the front line.Medical students should not study medicine in order to distance themselves from people. They should understand that medicine brings them closer to people, especially during moments of suffering and need.The second point is that fatigue and exhaustion can affect everyone. During war, the medical staff can be under enormous psychological and physical pressure. Misunderstandings can happen among staff members because everyone is stressed. Some healthcare workers may even lose relatives or be personally affected by the war. However, despite these circumstances, we still need self-control. We need to continue providing care with empathy, passion, and dedication.\n[28:26] Nizar Jaber: But war can make the treatment unavailable, this creates a gap between medical knowledge, and medical possibility. This is where World Health Organization reports, and Reuters' warnings about Lebanon's healthcare system, help contextualize this testimony. Shortages and system pressure are not just background conditions, they become part of the medical decision itself. Dr. Haydar's account also complicates the doctor-patient relationship. Shared decision making assumes that real choices exist, but in war, choices are reduced by the surrounding conditions. The physician may still want to respect the patient's autonomy, but the system limits what can actually be offered. This again connects to moral injury, it is being forced to practice medicine, below the standard one knows patients deserve. War does not only injure bodies, it injures the conditions that make care possible. After hearing from two physicians, the next question becomes educational. Are future doctors prepared for this kind of medicine? This led me to speak with Mohamad Jaber, a third year medical student at AUB. Mohamad Jaber's perspective introduces the medical student's position. Students are not yet fully physicians, but they are already being formed by what they witness. I asked him whether war made medical education feel more relevant, more insufficient, or both.\n[29:50] Nizar Jaber: How did the war affect the way you thought about medicine, and your future role as a physician?\n[29:57] Nizar Jaber: How did the war affect the way you thought about medicine and your future role as a physician?\n[30:03] Mohamad Jaber: The war made me realize that medicine is a social responsibility, more than just science. Before this crisis, medicine was centered on exams, rotations, which specialty you want to go into, and the clinical knowledge you have. But during the war, I began to see that being a physician also means working inside fragile systems, dealing with fear, scarcity, and public suffering. It's not only diagnosis and treatment, but also presence, communication, and sometimes witnessing suffering.\n[30:39] Nizar Jaber: Did the crisis make medical education feel more relevant, more insufficient, or both?\n[30:45] Mohamad Jaber: Well, actually, it made it seem like both. More relevant because concepts like trauma, triage, public health, ethics, emergency care, and communication suddenly became real rather than just theoretical concepts we talked about. But at the same time, it made it feel insufficient because medical education often assumes we're functioning in a hospital that's functional, with enough supplies, a safe environment, and enough time to do everything. But war exposed things that we students are not usually trained for: resource scarcity, moral distress, disrupted rotations, patient overflow, fear for one's own safety, as well as uncertainty.\n[31:24] Nizar Jaber: Are medical students prepared to think about triage, scarcity, and war medicine?\n[31:29] Mohamad Jaber: Mostly no, or only superficially. We do learn about triage, but it's very vague, the way we learn about it. And it's usually triage in a medical setting rather than in a trauma setting, for one. But also, the part of triage that we're not prepared for is the emotional aspect, the emotional weight of prioritizing a patient over another when everyone should receive the same level of care. But simply because of resources, we cannot.\n[31:55] Nizar Jaber: What should medical schools teach differently after this experience?\n[32:00] Mohamad Jaber: Medical schools should teach students not just how to triage and how to deal with trauma, because those are things that we learn every day. Medical school should teach students to reflect on the suffering, to reflect on what to learn from these experiences, to listen to patients' narratives, to understand the background behind what's happening, and to think of medicine as a part of society, not only as biomedical problem-solving and clinical diagnosis and management.\n[32:28] Nizar Jaber: Mohamad's answers show that war can make medical education feel both more urgent and more incomplete. It makes anatomy, trauma, ethics, and public health feel real, but it also reveals what is missing from ordinary training. This connects to Drs. Zgheib and Sabra's work on how sudden war affected medical education at AUB. Their account shows that war does not only affect patients and hospitals; it also affects curricula, student safety, clinical exposure, and the formation of future physicians. The student perspective shows that war becomes an unwanted teacher. It teaches scarcity, uncertainty, responsibility, and the limits of medicine before students have fully entered the profession. Medical schools should not expect students to become war doctors overnight, but they should prepare them to understand how medicine changes when ordinary systems fail.\n[33:22] Nizar Jaber: Taken together, these interviews show war medicine from four positions: the patient, the war surgeon, the local physician, and the medical student. Across these interviews, a common theme emerges. War medicine is not simply about treating war injuries. It is about what happens when the conditions of ordinary care are damaged. The civilian interview shows the patient's experience: fear, confusion, waiting, and the need to be recognized as a person. Dr. Abu-Sittah shows the broader ethical structure of war surgery: scarcity, triage, moral burden, and testimony. Dr. Haydar shows the local reality of medical practice under constraint in South Lebanon. Mohamad Jaber shows how war reshapes medical education and the identity of future physicians. Together, these perspectives show that war changes what doctors can offer, what patients can expect, and what students understand medicine to be.\n[34:19] Nizar Jaber: My central argument is that war medicine is not ordinary medical practice under difficult conditions. It is a structural transformation of medicine itself. Ethical priorities shift, clinical decision-making becomes collective, justice becomes practical, moral injury becomes more likely, patient narratives become harder to preserve, and medical education becomes unstable. This is why war medicine belongs to the medical humanities. It cannot be understood only through protocols, casualty numbers, or surgical outcomes. It requires attention to ethics, narrative, history, infrastructure, and lived experience.\n[34:59] Nizar Jaber: War reveals the fragility of ordinary medicine. In peacetime, we often take for granted that hospitals have supplies, that physicians have time, that patients can be transferred, and that ethical principles can be applied in orderly ways. War exposes how much care depends on systems outside the individual doctor-patient relationship. The lesson of war medicine is not only how to practice in crisis. It is also how urgently we must protect the conditions that make humane medicine possible.", "contentNote": "Contains discussion of war, mass-casualty events, civilian injuries, trauma, overwhelmed hospitals, and the psychological and ethical effects of conflict on patients and healthcare workers."});

